md('01-SECURITY-AND-AUTH.md','''# Security and authentication remediation

SEC-002 and DB-001 are fixed in the tested scope. SEC-001 and FE-002 remain partially fixed pending real Google acceptance; SEC-003 remains partial because access tokens are still JavaScript-readable.

Password recovery now conditionally consumes an unused, unexpired token inside the password/session/revocation transaction. Actual gateway/Auth/PostgreSQL tests produced one200 and seven400 responses for eight concurrent requests; replay failed, prior access/refresh credentials were denied and the winning password worked. New tokens are digests at rest. Existing unexpired64-hex legacy links remain accepted for their original one-hour window; submitting a stored digest is rejected. The fixture seeded a challenge in owned PostgreSQL; it did not verify external email delivery.

Admin creation uses UUIDs and plain INSERT. Save is update-only and creation timestamps are retained. SQLite and PostgreSQL tests preserve16 concurrent accounts; duplicate normalized email returns409 without changing the original identity/role. A database unique index also rejects case variants even when application normalization is bypassed.

OAuth uses S256 PKCE, browser state, nonce-bound signed Google ID tokens, issuer/audience/expiry/verified-email checks and the same account/MFA requirements as the main sign-in flow. Unconfigured OAuth returns503 instead of using dummy credentials. Redirects use the configured frontend origin, token fragments are removed immediately, and provider exception text is not logged. Cryptographic tests use local ephemeral RSA keys and handler tests explicitly stub Google/database transport. Those tests do not establish a successful external Google sign-in or provider replay acceptance. Review official [Google OpenID Connect guidance](https://developers.google.com/identity/openid-connect/openid-connect) when configuring the approved test client.

Browser refresh credentials now use an HttpOnly cookie, Secure in production and SameSite=Lax. Cookie-based refresh checks Origin; legacy explicit refresh-token API clients remain compatible. PostgreSQL consumes a refresh session conditionally so replay loses. The browser coalesces renewal and prevents a late401 from replaying a request under a newer login. Actual browser renewal, cookie removal at logout and reload without automatic sign-in pass. Failed server logout stays visible and is not reported as successful sign-out. HTTP native tests do not prove production Secure/TLS behavior. Access localStorage/cookie storage still requires an explicit XSS/session architecture decision.

## Migration and rollout requirements

Run the existing schema-owner Python migration job, including `202610210001_admin_identity.sql`/idempotent initializer. Before deployment, an authorized operator must inspect `SELECT LOWER(TRIM(email)),COUNT(*) FROM admin_users GROUP BY LOWER(TRIM(email)) HAVING COUNT(*)>1;`. Collisions deliberately fail migration; do not auto-merge privileged accounts. Existing staff IDs stay unchanged; new IDs are UUID-based. Auth changes need no new columns. Back up before migration and exercise it on a legacy-data clone. No production schema operation was run here.

Old vulnerable images must not become approved rollback targets after a security release. Keep the original AI encryption keyring/identities across replacements. The root fixture was restarted and retained SQL state and refresh access; container/TLS/legacy migration and real approved-provider acceptance remain separate launch gates.

Evidence: [security](evidence/runtime/security-acceptance.json), [browser renewal/logout](evidence/runtime/billing-browser.json), [restart](evidence/runtime/native-restart.json), [verification](evidence/verification.json).
''')
md('02-DOCUMENTS-AND-BILLING.md','''# Document, MFA and financial correctness

FE-004 stores a validated `canvasMode` with the existing session-scoped layout, alongside literal elements, overrides and page dimensions. Literal templates no longer regenerate a built-in base after reload. Old layouts default to template mode. Local history retains mode; server undo/redo flush pending canvas writes first. Document generation preserves literal presentation. A transition regression caught an incorrectly reset mode during implementation; the corrected17 targeted tests pass. Real Apply -> save -> same-session reload passes. Already-lost snapshots cannot be recovered from absent data. Literal snapshots do not acquire automatic semantic bindings.

FE-003 preserves `twoFactorEnabled` on profile reads and saves, and profile updates use the complete backend response. Actual enrollment and profile reload now show enabled MFA. Regular user/admin ownership checks remain enforced by the owning services.

FE-001/FE-005 remove the fixed paid invoice and fake download alert. Billing loads persisted history regardless of plan, initializes entitlements on entry and has loading, unavailable/retry, empty and pagination states. Paid provider invoices, pending payments, failed/refunded entries are labeled from recorded data; a plan override never creates a receipt. A real pro account with zero invoice rows shows no transactions. That test does not create synthetic paid records or prove checkout/webhook/receipt acceptance. Approved Paddle/PayHere positive transactions, downloads, refunds and reconciliation remain required.

API-004 maps subscription amount, currency and cadence from persisted billing facts. Unknown facts stay null; current catalog monthly prices and guessed USD are no longer substituted. USD/LKR minor-unit tests use explicit synthetic values and prove formatting/contract behavior only. Admin invoice views remove dollar/default-currency guesses. Other currencies need explicit unit metadata and reconciliation rather than guessing.

API-002 removes four unused missing-path wrappers. API-003 returns sanitized503 reporting errors and propagates failures through affected admin breakdown consumers. Isolated outage fixtures now assert503 instead of empty200. Healthy real backend browser navigation is tested separately; not every reporting fallback/action is certified.

The normal signed PDF service still returns readiness503 and render500 on this host. Its sandbox remains enabled. Browser rasterization tests use the explicit test-only browser launcher; this is not production PDF-sandbox certification.

Evidence: [custom reload](evidence/runtime/custom-template-reload.json), [main browser matrix](evidence/runtime/browser-tests.json), [billing](evidence/runtime/billing-browser.json), [PDF](evidence/runtime/pdf-readiness.json), [runtime API checks](evidence/runtime/integrated-tests.json).
''')
md('03-AI-QUALITY.md',f'''# Actual model quality and routing

**AI-001 remains not fixed.** Fresh real CPU inference completed against the same112-case corpus and the current seven MCP parameter schemas, independently compared with the baseline. No provider response mocks are counted as model intelligence. These are proposals; they are not112 committed document edits.

| Run | Passed / total | p50 / p95 ms | Scope |
|---|---:|---:|---|
| Qwen3 1.7B Q4_K_M | {stats(a1)['passed']}/112 | {stats(a1)['p50_ms']} / {stats(a1)['p95_ms']} | context8192, output768, thinking off |
| Qwen3 4B Q4_K_M | {stats(a4)['passed']}/16 | {stats(a4)['p50_ms']} / {stats(a4)['p95_ms']} | bounded one-per-category sample, context4096, four threads |
| Extra business details, 1.7B | {stats(extra)['passed']}/8 | {stats(extra)['p50_ms']} / {stats(extra)['p95_ms']} | addresses, contacts, dates, exact legal-name capitalization, notes/payment terms and tax/discount/currency |

The larger sample has different context/thread settings and shared-host load. It is not a controlled ranking. All failures and raw actual outputs remain in evidence. Schema/evidence/mapping failures and timeouts are functional failures even where safe rejection protects the database. No schema or authorization checks were relaxed to increase a score. The supplement's interrupted preflight trial was stopped and rerun with sufficient measured headroom; no interrupted case is counted as successful.

A separately configured16384-context1.7B candidate passed actual admin connectivity and a full gateway/Chat/signed-admin/Ollama/SSE question in38525ms, with one inference ledger entry and unchanged document slots. An8192 conservative byte-budget attempt was refused with CONTEXT_LIMIT before dispatch; its evidence is retained. This explains a configuration limit, not a quality fix. A zero request budget yielded AI_BUDGET_EXCEEDED with zero dispatch; disabling the provider yielded POLICY_CHANGED with zero dispatch. Actual free/pro model assignments propagated through the signed runtime API without redeployment. A tiny connectivity tool does not certify production extraction. The4B bounded context is not approved for full Chat prompts.

Only downloaded task-owned models and a loopback-only Ollama process were used, one loaded model/parallel inference at a time. No new large model or production service was installed. Models were unloaded between candidates. Shared-host memory guards constrain these tests; complete target-VPS/CPU/container/PDF+LLM queue benchmarks and real approved cloud fallback/cost remain blocked. Users continue to see DocCraft AI Assistant, and provider credentials remain server-side.

Next: evaluate a capable model/constrained-generation configuration on agreed hardware, preserve evidence/ownership schemas, rerun the fixed corpus plus applied browser conversations, and require actual field/math/undo/reload/PDF correctness before activation. Do not approve either current candidate from a health probe.

Evidence: [small actual results](evidence/ai/validated-1.7b.json), [larger actual results](evidence/ai/validated-4b.json), [business cases](evidence/ai/business-supplement/scenarios.json), [business results](evidence/ai/business-supplement/validated.json), [current schema comparison](evidence/ai/current-schema-comparison.json), [actual Chat/budgets](evidence/runtime/live-ai-integration.json), [assignment propagation](evidence/runtime/plan-models.json).
''')
md('04-DEPLOYMENT-AND-RECOVERY.md','''# Deployment, operations and recovery

**No production infrastructure, deployment, credentials or repository settings were changed.** Terraform formatting/validation and Actionlint pass. Deployment/migration/secret/network policies have33 local tests. These include simulated controller execution and transport stubs; they are not successful Docker/GHCR/SSH deployments. The development CPU is x86_64; the operator has not supplied and accepted a final VPS/CPU target.

Docker daemon/final application image builds, architecture/runtime networking/cgroups/TLS/restarts, secure PDF sandbox and actual migration/rollback are blocked. Provider-specific Terraform/remote state, reviewed plans and GitHub environment/registry/forced-SSH acceptance also remain blocked. Existing vulnerable-base advisories remain unresolved; no digest was replaced without rebuild acceptance. Keep both production enable gates disabled.

Available commands, after operator bootstrap on disposable staging:

```sh
python3 deploy/scripts/validate-config.sh  # use the script's documented interpreter/arguments; see note below
python3 deploy/scripts/check-migrations.py
python3 -m unittest discover -s deploy/tests
terraform fmt -check -recursive terraform
terraform -chdir=terraform init -backend=false
terraform -chdir=terraform validate
actionlint
docker compose --env-file <private-nonsecret-release-env> -f deploy/docker/compose.production.yml config --quiet
```

`validate-config.sh` is a shell wrapper: invoke `sh deploy/scripts/validate-config.sh` with the configuration arguments from `deploy/scripts/configure.py --help`. Never print rendered secret-bearing Compose configuration. Build all `Backend.Dockerfile`, `Python.Dockerfile` and `Frontend.Dockerfile` targets for the approved CPU using the existing image workflow, scan final layers, then run actual private-service/health/failure/migration/compatible-rollback acceptance. The actual release manifest and private setup are prerequisites, not values fabricated by this audit. Application rollback must preserve schema/keyrings and must never automatically restore a database or reopen vulnerabilities from an old image.

## Optional encrypted off-host backup

New `backup_remote.py`, service and hourly timer are deliberately uninstalled. Install a reviewed restic binary separately. Prepare `/etc/doccraft/backup.json` from the example, root-owned0600 repository/password/credential files, an independently initialized encrypted remote repository and least-privilege credentials. Set `maintenance_approved` only after accepting the outage window. The job shares the deployment lock, checks writers are running, stops Document/Admin writers briefly, validates a consistent database dump and uploads DB, both application volumes, configuration/runtime and original secret/key material. It restarts writers in finally and records success only after upload/check. It never initializes/rekeys, prunes or restores a database. The raw local dump stays in a restricted temporary directory and is removed afterward.

This source implementation has not executed against restic/off-host storage. Hourly scheduling plus upload time is not a proven one-hour RPO. Backup maintenance can interrupt writes; this is not zero downtime. Repository encryption/password recovery, immutable retention, least privilege, storage failure and full-host restore remain mandatory acceptance work. Password/recovery material needs a separately protected off-server escrow; an encrypted snapshot whose only password was lost with the VPS is not recovery.

A real native PostgreSQL18.4 snapshot restored73 tables and499 rows with matching content and568 constraint definitions. Dump242ms, restore685ms for the sampled synthetic dataset. A distinct test database was dropped after verification. It is not production PostgreSQL17/TLS/PITR, encrypted restic restore, legacy-data or full-host recovery. No automatic production data restoration occurred.

## Optional monitoring delivery

Prepare root-owned0600 `/etc/doccraft/monitoring.json` from the example. Configure an external heartbeat timeout and alert destination with a named operator. Monitoring now detects stale backups, missing/unhealthy required containers and additional filesystem pressure, and sends only bounded alert identifiers/time. The tests use explicit send stubs. Actual endpoint receipt, whole-host loss and disk-failure alerts are unverified. Monitoring and backup configuration are host-only private policies, not frontend or master container secrets.

Measured native workloads:96/96 edits at concurrency8 in9.070 seconds (nearest-rank p50/p95/p99=727/963/999ms) and480/480 at concurrency8 with one-second think time in111.637 seconds (801/1316/1826ms); zero model dispatch for those edits. Task-owned peak simultaneous PSS including Ollama was4786.35MiB on a15346.05MiB,8-logical-CPU x86_64 shared host; minimum observed host availability1437.18MiB. These peaks exclude browser/test/build/unrelated desktop processes. The8321-sample resource window includes idle periods, separate model corpora and a native restart; it is not a continuous mixed-user load test. Raw non-atomic CPU counter artifacts are retained; filtered stable-interval estimates peak near796% where one core=100%. Use cgroup accounting on target hardware rather than this estimate for capacity claims. Resource samples include the local model, but exclude unrelated desktop/browser/build processes and lack cgroup enforcement. They do not establish an8GB production minimum or sustainable mixed throughput. See resource evidence for actual peaks; do not purchase hardware from unit tests.

Restore acceptance order: approved host/network/runtime; recover original keys/config; restore to an isolated database and volumes from one consistent snapshot; verify schema epoch, hashes and ownership; deploy only compatible patched digests; run user/admin/AI/PDF/billing checks; then obtain operator authorization for traffic. Repeat a complete off-host drill and accept RPO/RTO before launch.

Evidence: [verification](evidence/verification.json), [restore](evidence/runtime/backup-restore.json), [native restart](evidence/runtime/native-restart.json), [resources](evidence/resource-summary.json), [PDF](evidence/runtime/pdf-readiness.json).
'''.replace('python3 deploy/scripts/validate-config.sh  # use the script\'s documented interpreter/arguments; see note below','sh deploy/scripts/validate-config.sh --help'))
